      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-334660",
      ":question/problem": "A body of mass $10kg$ falls vertically downward. Let $v(t)$ be the body's velocity, in meters per second, $t \\ge 0$ seconds after the fall began, with downward taken as the positive direction.\nThe air resistance on the body is proportional to the square of the body's velocity and acts in the direction opposite to motion.\n\nWhich differential equation best models the velocity? Assume that $k > 0$ is a constant of proportionality, the acceleration due to gravity is $g$, and the only forces acting on the body are the weight due to gravity and drag."
