      ":question/problem": "A container of mass $10\\,\\text{kg}$ falls vertically downward. Let $v(t)$ be the body's velocity, in meters per second, $t\\ge 0$ seconds after the fall began, with downward taken as the positive direction.\nThe air resistance on the container is proportional to the square of its velocity with a constant of proportionality $k>0$ and acts in the direction opposite to motion.\nAt a particular moment during the fall, the container's velocity is $5\\,\\text{m/s}$ and instantaneous acceleration is $4.8\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$\n\nFind the constant of proportionality $k,$ and the differential equation that best models the velocity.\nAssume the acceleration due to gravity is $g=9.8\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2},$ and the only forces acting on the container are weight due to gravity and drag.\n\n$\\,$ The constant of proportionality is $k=$\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ The differential equation that best models the velocity is $\\frac{dv}{dt}=$\n{{field-3}}."
